The real-program pinning check passed: a fresh auxiliary claim showed that loading the regenerated `FuncDef` creates exactly the closure used by the entry claim, and the parsed source/claim bodies are constructor-identical after the list-unit normalization K applies. The static audit found no candidate-local proof rules or oracles, but it found a material fixed-model mismatch on the used `lowerC` rule: valid Unicode inputs `"éÉ"` and `"İ"` produce opposite K-versus-CPython counts (2 vs 1, and 1 vs 2). I’ve preserved both executable witnesses and the exhaustive 1,096-entry rule/declaration inventory.
Stages 4 and 5 are complete. The claim mechanically pins the submitted body and is result-constraining under the supplied model, but the used ASCII-only lowerC rule yields false real-Python results on valid Unicode source-domain witnesses. The exhaustive source inventory and used-construct map are preserved.
